Vendored subset of pysteps — the Lucas-Kanade motion estimator
==============================================================

This directory contains a redistribution of code from the pysteps project
(https://github.com/pySTEPS/pysteps), licensed under the BSD 3-Clause
License — see LICENSE-pysteps in this directory.

Vendored version
----------------
pysteps 1.21.1 (see `_VENDORED_FROM` in __init__.py), taken from the PyPI
source distribution `pysteps-1.21.1.tar.gz`.

Why vendored
------------
Two reasons, and the second is the one that decided it:

1. pysteps is not installable on Home Assistant OS (no wheels for
   `musllinux_*_cp314`, and the source build wants an OpenMP toolchain
   HA OS does not ship at runtime) — the same wall that produced the
   sibling `_vendor/pysteps_steps` tree.
2. Neither the sidecar image nor the algorithm library depends on
   pysteps, and Phase H needs exactly ONE pysteps function: `dense_
   lucaskanade`, as the literature's reference motion estimator, so that
   the Farnebäck field the service serves can be scored against it on
   identical Layer A cases (`forecast_skill_plan.md` §3, H-c and H-F).
   Adding pysteps and its dependency tree to a memory-capped VM image to
   call one function that will probably never be served is a bad trade;
   six source files are not.

Separate from `_vendor/pysteps_steps` on purpose
------------------------------------------------
That tree is the strict subset `pysteps.nowcasts.steps.forecast`
executes, identified by an `sys.settrace` audit, and it carries seven
modifications made for peak memory because STEPS runs those functions
sixteen times per timestep. None of that is true of this tree, which is
essentially unmodified. Merging them would make it impossible to say
which statement applies to a given file.

Files vendored
--------------
Six upstream source files, mirroring the upstream layout, plus package
`__init__.py` files:

    decorators.py              from pysteps/decorators.py
    exceptions.py              from pysteps/exceptions.py
    motion/lucaskanade.py      from pysteps/motion/lucaskanade.py
    tracking/lucaskanade.py    from pysteps/tracking/lucaskanade.py
    feature/shitomasi.py       from pysteps/feature/shitomasi.py
    utils/cleansing.py         from pysteps/utils/cleansing.py
    utils/images.py            from pysteps/utils/images.py
    utils/interpolate.py       from pysteps/utils/interpolate.py

All upstream docstrings are kept verbatim, including the references
(Bouguet 2001; Lucas and Kanade 1981; Shi and Tomasi 1994).

Modifications from upstream
---------------------------
1. All `from pysteps.X import Y` rewritten to the equivalent relative
   import (`from ..utils.cleansing import ...`). Mechanical; no logic
   changes.
2. `motion/lucaskanade.py` — the two runtime dispatchers,
   `pysteps.feature.get_method(fd_method)` and
   `pysteps.utils.get_method(interp_method)`, are replaced by the
   `_FEATURE_METHODS` / `_INTERP_METHODS` dicts and their two lookup
   helpers. Upstream's dispatchers import every detector and utility in
   the package (`blob` → scikit-image, `tstorm` → pandas, and the
   `motion` interface → the `_vet` / `_proesmans` Cython extensions),
   which is the whole of what vendoring is meant to avoid. Only the two
   DEFAULT methods are vendored — `fd_method="shitomasi"` and
   `interp_method="idwinterp2d"` — and any other name now raises a
   `ValueError` naming what is available instead of an ImportError from
   three files away. The call sites and the arguments are unchanged.
3. `decorators.py` — `postprocess_import` and `deprecate_args` dropped
   (importer-only helpers; nothing on the LK path calls them). The three
   that are used — `check_input_frames`, `prepare_interpolator`,
   `memoize` — and their shared `_add_extra_kwrds_to_docstrings` are
   verbatim.
4. `utils/interpolate.py` — `rbfinterp2d` and its `_Rbf_cached` companion
   dropped, with the now-unused `scipy.interpolate.Rbf` and `warnings`
   imports. `idwinterp2d` (the default, and the only one this repo asks
   for) and `_cKDTree_cached` are verbatim.
5. `exceptions.py` — kept whole; it is four exception classes and no
   code, and a copy is cheaper than a cross-tree import into
   `pysteps_steps`, which would couple two vendored trees that document
   different provenance guarantees.

No numerical behaviour is changed by any of the above: with the default
arguments this tree runs the identical code path as an installed pysteps
1.21.1. `tests/test_flow_variants.py` pins the two properties that
matter — that a known synthetic displacement comes back within 15 %, and
that the axis/sign convention of the returned field matches
`dense_flow`'s `(vy, vx)`.

Convention note for callers
---------------------------
`dense_lucaskanade` returns `(2, m, n)` with `out[0]` the x- (column,
eastward) component and `out[1]` the y- (row, southward) component, in
pixels per timestep. `dmi_nowcast_core.dense_flow.dense_flow` returns
`(vy, vx)` — row component first. `flow_variants.lucaskanade_flow` does
that swap, and a test pins it.

